  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Labour market information system strenghtened and disseminate information on national labour market trends</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Namibia has not recently conducted major surveys, to support evidence-based planning and decision making. The 2021 National Census was postponed several times was finally conducted in 2023 due to limited financial resources following the Covid-19 pandemic, among other reasons. The Census preliminary results have just been published showing a significant increase in population since the census of 1991 at independence. The population now stands at 3.02 million people. The population has grown almost twofold since the first post-independence census was conducted in 1991. Females make up a greater proportion of the population in the 2023 PHC as has been the trend for the past four censuses. The population is growing, at an increasing rate compared with previous censuses. Average household size, which has been on the decline since 1991, is 3.8 persons per household. 
Namibia&#x2019;s Statistics Agency (NSA) has reported that several vital statistics are outdated, including the labor force survey, national population and housing census statistics, household income and expenditure statistics, financial inclusion data, migration data, land statistics, data on mortality and causes of deaths, social inclusion statistics, GDP rebasing (revision), CPI rebasing, and data on demography and health variables. Significantly labour market information is lagging with the last Labour Force Survey having been conducted in 2018, the National Household Income and Expenditure survey in 2016, Migration Data in 2015, Mortality and Causes of Deaths in 2016, et al. The National and Housing Census results are a major milestone towards addressing the range of data deficits. 
